¶
'''Botaurinæ''' (bō-tâ-rī′nē), ''n. pl.'' [NL., < ''Botaurus'' + ''-inæ''.] A subfamily of ''Ardeidæ'', or herons, containing the bitterns, distinguished from true herons by having only ten tail-feathers and two pairs of powder-down tracts, and the outer toe shorter than the inner. {{smaller|In habits the ''Botaurinæ'' also differ from the other ''Ardeidæ'', being solitary, nesting on the ground, and laying eggs unlike those of true herons. See cut under ''bittern''.}}

'''Botaurus''' (bō-tâ′rus), ''n.'' [NL., irreg. < L. ''bos'', an ox, + ''taurus'', a bull; suggested by the old form (ME. ''butor'', OF. ''butor'', ''botor'') of ''bittern''<sup>1</sup>, q. v.] The typical genus of the subfamily ''Botaurinæ''. See ''bittern''<sup>1</sup>.

'''botch'''<sup>1</sup>† (boch), ''n.'' [< ME. ''botche'', ''bocche'', < OF. ''boche'', a botch, sore, var. of ''boce'', a botch, swelling, > mod. F. ''bosse'', E. ''boss''<sup>1</sup>: see ''boss''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. OD. ''butse'', a boil, swelling, < ''butsen'', D. ''botsen'', strike, beat, akin to OHG. ''bōzan'' = E. ''beat''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''botch''<sup>2</sup>.] A swelling on the skin; a large ulcerous affection; a boil.
{{smaller block|<poem>Yet who more foul, disrobed of attire?
Pearl’d with the ''botch'' as children burnt with fire.</poem>
{{right|''Middleton'', Micro-Cynicon, i. 3.}}
''Botches'' and blains must all his flesh emboss.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., xii. 180.}}}}

