{{smaller block|Fancy the most assiduous potter, but without his wheel; reduced to make dishes, or rather amorphous ''botches'', by mere kneading and baking.
{{right|''Carlyle.''}}}}
{{smaller block|A poorly paid teacher, whose work is a ''botch'', and therefore an injury to the growing mind.
{{right|''Jour. of Education'', XIX. 41.}}}}

'''4.''' A bungling, unskilful workman or operator of any kind; a botcher.

'''botchedly''' (boch′ed-li or bocht′li), ''adv.'' [< ''botched'', pp. of ''botch''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.'', + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] In a botched or clumsy manner; with botches or patches.
{{smaller block|Thus patch they heaven, more ''botch’dly'' then old clothes.
{{right|''Dr. H. More'', Psychathanasia, III. iii. 67.}}}}

'''botcher<sup>1</sup>''' (boch′ėr), ''n.'' [< ME. *''bocchere'' (spelled ''bochchare'', Prompt. Parv.); < ''botch''<sup>2</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' A mender; a repairer or patcher; specifically, a tailor who does repairing.
{{smaller block|Let the ''botcher'' mend him: Anything that’s mended is but patched.
{{right|''Shak.'', T. N., i. 5.}}}}
{{smaller block|Physicians are the body’s cobblers, rather the ''botchers'' of men’s bodies; as the one patches our tatter’d clothes, so the other solders our diseased flesh.
{{right|''Ford'', Lover’s Melancholy, i. 2.}}}}
'''2.''' One who botches; a clumsy, bungling workman; a bungler.

'''botcher<sup>2</sup>''' (boch′ėr), ''n.'' [Origin unknown.] The grilse: a local English name in the Severn valley.

'''botcherly''' (boch′ėr-li), ''a.'' [< ''botcher''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ly''<sup>1</sup>.] Clumsy; unworkmanlike. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|''Botcherly'' mingle-mangle of collections.
{{right|''Hartlib'', tr. of Comenius, p. 30.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Botcherly'' poetry, ''botcherly!''
{{right|''Middleton and Rowley'', Spanish Gypsy, ii. 1.}}}}

