{{smaller block|Youre ''bother'' love [the love of you both].
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, iv. 168.}}
And Abraham took sheep and oxen, and gave them unto Abimelech; and ''both'' of them made a covenant.
{{right|Gen. xxi. 27.}}
He will not bear the loss of his rank, because he can bear the loss of his estate; but he will bear ''both'', because he is prepared for ''both''. {{right|''Bolingbroke''.}}
''Both'' had been presidents, ''both'' had lived to great age, ''both'' were early patriots, and ''both'' were distinguished and ever honored by their immediate agency in the act of independence. {{right|''D. Webster'', Adams and Jefferson.}}
[The genitive ''both’s'' (ME. ''bothes'', ''bothers'', earlier ''bother'', ''bathre'') is now disused; in the earlier period it was joined usually with the genitive plural of the personal pronoun. Subsequently the simple ''both'', equivalent to ''of both'', was used.}}
